The Price of Learning:
 Experimental Evidence on Agricultural Technology Trials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Free distribution of new agricultural inputs,
 often through trial packs,
 is widely used to encourage smallholder farmers to experiment with unfamiliar technologies before deciding whether to adopt them.
 Yet critics argue that charging even a modest price may improve the effectiveness of such interventions by screening for interested users,
 increasing commitment through sunk costs,
 or signaling product quality.
 This raises a practical question for program design:
 should trial packs be distributed free of charge,
 or does charging a positive price make such interventions more effective?
 We address this question in a field experiment among smallholder farmers in Uganda who were offered trial packs of a recently released maize variety.
 Our design separately identifies the screening,
 sunk-cost,
 and signaling effects of price.
 We find that willingness to pay predicts subsequent use of the trial pack,
 suggesting that it can help identify farmers most likely to benefit.
 Charging a positive price,
 however,
 neither increases adoption in the following season nor do we find evidence that it signals product quality.
 Instead,
 farmers who received seed for free managed the trial more carefully.
 Although charging a positive price does not increase adoption,
 higher willingness to pay predicts higher production in the following season,
 likely because most farmers recycle seed from their trial harvest.
 For policy,
 the value of charging a modest price lies in screening,
 not in stimulating adoption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Keywords:
 Technology adoption;
 Trial packs;
 Screening;
 Sunk cost;
 Price signaling.
\end_layout

