\begin_layout Subsubsection
Adoption,
 input use,
 and production in subsequent season
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adoption of the promoted technology in the subsequent season is particularly relevant,
 as a primary aim of seed trial packs is to encourage experimentation that may lead to continued use.
 Observing behavior in the following season provides an early indication of whether farmers’ trial experiences translate into repeated adoption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two adoption measures mirror the intention outcomes from the previous section but capture actual behavior on the randomly selected plot:
 whether seed of an improved variety was used (hybrid or OPV),
 and whether the promoted Bazooka variety was planted.
 We also examine whether farmers applied fertilizer or crop protection chemicals on the randomly selected plot,
 mirroring the complementary input measures from the trial pack season.
 Finally,
 we measure production and productivity on the randomly selected plot,
 both transformed using the inverse hyperbolic sine (IHS) to accommodate zero values while approximating a logarithmic transformation for larger values.
 As before,
 we aggregate outcomes into a Subsequent Season Index following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "anderson2008multiple"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Results (in Table 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{tab:Effects-on-Adoption}
\end_layout

\end_inset

) reveal a nuanced pattern.
 Adoption measures show no significant treatment effects:
 neither willingness to pay (screening) nor the initial offer price (signaling) is associated with the likelihood of using improved or promoted seed in the following season,
 and complementary input use on the randomly selected plot does not differ across pricing arms.
 However,
 screening effects on production are significant and substantial:
 Farmers with higher willingness to pay achieved significantly higher production on the randomly selected plot in the subsequent season.
 The association is economically meaningful:
 a one-standard-deviation increase in willingness to pay (about
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$wtp_sd}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 shillings) is associated with roughly
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$prod_per_sd}
\end_layout

\end_inset

% higher production,
 and moving from the 25th to the 75th percentile of willingness to pay (
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$wtp_p25}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 to
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$wtp_p75}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 shillings) with about
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$prod_per_iqr}
\end_layout

\end_inset

% more.
 Sunk cost effects on productivity are also significant but negative,
 mirroring the pattern observed during the trial season:
 farmers who paid for the trial pack produced about
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
Sexpr{mag$prodv_sunk}
\end_layout

\end_inset

% less per unit of land than those who received it for free.
 This productivity effect is robust to wild cluster bootstrap and randomization inference,
 although it does not survive a familywise correction across the outcomes in this family (Appendix
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Appendix-inference"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The production association should nonetheless be interpreted with care.
 When we add baseline wealth and liquidity proxies to the specification (Appendix
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Appendix-controls"

\end_inset

),
 the screening coefficient on trial-pack management is essentially unchanged,
 but the coefficient on subsequent production attenuates from 0.060 to 0.037 (p=0.055).
 Part of the association between willingness to pay and later production may thus reflect the resources of high-valuation farmers rather than private information about returns alone.
 Screening on resources and screening on returns are both relevant for targeting,
 but they carry different policy implications.
 The sunk-cost estimates are unaffected by these controls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The combination of null adoption effects and significant production and productivity effects may be explained through seed recycling.
 Among farmers who grew the promoted Bazooka variety on the randomly selected plot in the subsequent season (69% of the sample),
 83% used recycled seed from the harvested grain obtained from the seed trial pack,
 rather than freshly purchased certified seed (despite the fact that a hybrid variety such as Bazooka should not be reused).
 Recycling rates did not differ across treatment arms (84% among discounted versus 82% among full-price farmers,
 p=0.48),
 confirming that the production differences are not driven by differential adoption of the variety.
 Moreover,
 the screening effect on production remains significant when the sample is restricted to Bazooka recyclers (p=0.031),
 indicating that the effect operates within the group of farmers who recycle the seed trial pack variety.
 This recycling pattern is itself policy-relevant.
 A central rationale for distributing trial packs is to seed durable demand for certified seed,
 yet only about one in six continuing farmers returned to the market for fresh certified seed,
 even though hybrid maize loses vigor when its grain is replanted;
 in our setting the packs largely seeded a recycling habit rather than repeat purchase.
 This shifts where the payoff to the intervention accrues,
 from repeat certified-seed sales to the quality of the seed farmers save and recycle,
 and it cautions that the productivity gains we document may erode over successive recycling cycles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most likely channel through which pricing affects the quality of recycled seed is through first-season management practices.
 For the sunk cost channel specifically,
 farmers who paid for the trial pack saved significantly less grain as seed for the following season (p=0.039) and were less likely to keep the trial harvest separate from other varieties during the trial season (Table
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
ref{tab:Effects-on-Use-sep}
\end_layout

\end_inset

),
 both of which would reduce the purity and quality of recycled seed.
 For the screening channel,
 higher willingness to pay selected farmers who managed the trial more carefully across multiple dimensions,
 including correct planting,
 complementary input use,
 and post-harvest separation.
 While we cannot isolate which specific management practice drives the subsequent production effect,
 the evidence is consistent with the view that pricing shapes first-season seed management in ways that affect the quality of recycled seed available for subsequent planting.
\end_layout

