\begin_layout Section
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this paper,
 we asked whether trial packs of a new agricultural technology should be distributed free of charge or whether charging a positive price makes such interventions more effective.
 We addressed this question using a field experiment with smallholder maize farmers in Uganda and a three-stage pricing design that separately identifies the screening,
 sunk-cost,
 and signaling effects of price.
 This allows us to move beyond the average effect of charging for a trial pack and identify the distinct mechanisms through which prices shape farmers' behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our results show that these mechanisms point in different directions.
 First,
 we find no evidence that higher prices signal superior product quality or otherwise strengthen farmers' beliefs about the technology.
 It should be noted that all experimental prices were at or below the prevailing retail price and were offered through a credible project channel,
 so this null does not rule out price-quality inference in anonymous market transactions,
 where concerns about counterfeit inputs are most salient.
 Second,
 willingness to pay consistently predicts careful use of the trial pack,
 investment in complementary inputs,
 and subsequent production,
 indicating that prices screen for farmers who are better positioned to benefit from a new technology.
 Third,
 sunk-cost effects run counter to conventional expectations:
 farmers who received the seed free of charge managed the trial more carefully than those who paid for it.
 Rather than reinforcing commitment,
 payment appears to have reduced experimentation and learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These findings highlight that the effects of price are not monolithic.
 A single price simultaneously determines who receives a technology,
 how recipients engage with it,
 and how much they learn from the experience.
 More broadly,
 our results extend the literature on pricing and technology adoption 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
before "eg."
key "10.1257/aer.100.5.2383,10.1162/qjec.2010.125.1.1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 by showing that prices simultaneously affect selection into use,
 learning during experimentation,
 and subsequent productivity.
 Focusing only on overall adoption therefore obscures distinct mechanisms that may offset one another.
 In our setting,
 the positive screening effect of price is offset by a negative effect on experimentation,
 while signaling plays little role.
 This decomposition helps explain why charging for trial packs does not increase subsequent adoption despite identifying farmers who ultimately achieve higher productivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results also have broader implications for the design of technology promotion programs.
 Our findings speak directly to the emerging literature on self-targeting agricultural subsidies 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "duchoslav2022guiding,banda2022pricing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These proposals rely on the premise that willingness to pay identifies farmers with the highest expected returns to a technology.
 Our results provide revealed-preference evidence supporting this premise:
 farmers with higher willingness to pay not only express stronger demand but also manage the technology more carefully and ultimately achieve higher production.
 That said,
 willingness to pay elicited from farmers bidding with their own money partly reflects wealth and liquidity rather than expected returns alone:
 controlling for baseline wealth and liquidity proxies leaves the screening effect on trial management essentially unchanged,
 but attenuates the association with subsequent production.
 Screening on resources and screening on returns have different policy implications,
 and our design cannot fully separate the two.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At the same time,
 the negative sunk-cost effects suggest that requiring payment may undermine one of the principal objectives of trial packs:
 encouraging farmers to experiment,
 observe,
 and learn under low-risk conditions.
 These results therefore point toward a hybrid approach,
 in which willingness to pay is elicited to identify motivated users while the trial pack itself is distributed at a low or zero transaction price.
 Such an approach preserves the targeting benefits of pricing without sacrificing the learning benefits of free provision.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taken together,
 our findings suggest that prices are not simply a tool for rationing demand or recovering costs.
 They simultaneously determine who experiments with a technology,
 how that experimentation unfolds,
 and who ultimately benefits from it.
 By separating screening,
 sunk-cost,
 and signaling effects,
 this paper shows that these mechanisms need not reinforce one another.
 In our setting,
 the value of price lies primarily in identifying farmers most likely to benefit rather than in stimulating adoption itself.
 This perspective helps reconcile the mixed evidence from the pricing literature by showing that prices simultaneously affect who adopts,
 how farmers learn,
 and how technologies are ultimately used.
\end_layout

