\begin_layout Standard
Because farmers bid with their own money,
 the negotiated price may partly reflect wealth,
 liquidity,
 or bargaining ability rather than valuation.
 To assess whether this drives the screening estimates,
 we re-estimate the main specifications adding a vector of baseline wealth and liquidity proxies.
 Table
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:controls-robustness"

\end_inset

 compares the estimates with and without these controls for the three family indices and the individual outcomes emphasized in the main text.
 The screening effect on the Trial Seed Use Index is essentially unchanged,
 as are all sunk-cost coefficients.
 The screening coefficients on adoption intentions and on subsequent production attenuate but retain their sign and order of magnitude,
 suggesting that part of the association between willingness to pay and later outcomes reflects the resources of high-valuation farmers.
 We interpret this pattern in the main text.
\end_layout

