\begin_layout Standard
The surprise discount that identifies the sunk-cost effect is randomized at the village level,
 with 38 discounted and 38 non-discounted villages.
 With this number of clusters,
 standard cluster-robust inference can understate uncertainty,
 and Table 1 shows two covariates (household size and male headship) that differ across discount arms.
 This appendix therefore re-examines inference on the sunk-cost coefficients using a restricted wild cluster bootstrap,
 randomization inference that permutes the discount assignment across villages,
 and specifications that control for the imbalanced covariates.
 Table
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:inference-robustness"

\end_inset

 reports the results.
 The three sunk-cost results that are significant under cluster-robust standard errors (the Trial Seed Use Index,
 keeping the harvest separate,
 and productivity in the subsequent season) remain significant under all alternative procedures,
 with p-values between 0.009 and 0.018.
 All other sunk-cost coefficients are far from significance under every method.
 We also compute Romano-Wolf familywise-adjusted p-values across the individual outcomes within each family:
 the effect on keeping the harvest separate survives at the 10 percent level (p=0.078),
 while the productivity effect,
 though robust in isolation,
 does not clear the familywise bar against the other five outcomes in its family (p=0.111).
 For completeness,
 the same familywise correction applied to the screening margin leaves the effects on plans to use an improved variety (p=0.030) and on subsequent production (p=0.023) significant.
\end_layout

